concepts.content:
When working with two-dimensional (2D) images, partial derivatives can be approximated along the $n$ and $m$ components using filters like $[1, -1]^T$ and $[1, -1]$. However, these length-2 filters shift the output image by half a pixel. This spatial misalignment can be removed by using a rotated reference frame, as implemented in the **Roberts cross** operator:

$$
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 & ~0\\
  0 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
~~~~~~~
\begin{bmatrix}
  ~0 & 1 \\
  -1 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Introduced in 1963, this operator was designed for efficiency at a time when image processing was extremely computationally expensive.

